+
+### 8/28/26, 8:28 PM — Athanasius325 / Fr James
+
+It involves both transmission and duration. It's not enough that, for instance, the prior generation's Bishop of that diocese allowed liturgical dance. That does not make it, in any serious way, a "received tradition". 
+
+Yes: something can be received in one tradition and not received in another. I use liturgical dance as an example not because it's an inherently silly concept, but rather because it's a concept that is silly in most of the Western context. Liturgical dance is more properly utilized in, say, Eastern or African traditions of worship.
+
+Something like the Te Deum Laudamus, itself not directly from the Scriptures but from the early centuries of the Church (mid-to-late 4th century). I think every orthodox church tradition would allow for that as at least an acceptable Canticle. 
